foreign  [Music]  hit by an explosion a little earlier  today has warned a serious problems  Japan is scrambling to avert a looming  years old I visited India and I was  surprised to see that the power was shut  off for a few hours every single day at  the time my main concern was how do I  watch TV or how do I use the internet  when the power was off but looking back  these were the least of people's  concerns no power meant that  refrigerators couldn't be running and  that small businesses and hospitals had  to pay for generators on a daily basis  for many people living there this was  just the harsh reality of daily life  naturally you would assume that this is  only an issue in developing countries  but one developed country that's very  much facing the same issue is none other  than Japan Japan has not only had to  implement electricity saving measures  like turning down the heat and dimming  the lights but they've been experiencing  full-on blackouts the country with  internet speeds of 319 terabits per  second and that bullet trains capable of  reaching 603 kilometers per hour is  struggling to keep the lights on at  first glance you might be driven to  think that this is due to Japan's  economic situation after all it's no  secret that Japan has been facing an  economic crisis for decades they have a  debt to GDP ratio of 257 percent their  stock market hasn't made a new all-time  high in 33 years and the Japanese yen is  at 35-year lows against the US dollar  all of these would be great reasons for  Japan to cut back on power consumption  and to try to save money but their  economic crisis is ironically not the  main reason that they're also facing an  energy crisis in fact Japan would likely  be facing an energy crisis even if their  economy was booming the problem would  actually probably be worse because  people would likely consume even more  power if the economy was booming but  then if the problem isn't being able to  pay for it what is well for Japan the  problem is actually getting their hands  on electricity for decades 80 to 90  percent of of Japan's energy has been  imported for perspective only about  seven percent of America's energy is  imported this has become one of Japan's  biggest bottlenecks and despite their  best efforts they've been unable to  escape the shackles of energy dependence  but how did such an advanced and  developed country end up in a situation  where they can't even keep the lights on  taking a look back Japan's energy crisis  wasn't something that developed  overnight it's something that developed  over a century dating back to the 1800s  it all started with a man named Emperor  Meiji Who Rose to power in 1868 when he  took over much of Japan was filled with  a bunch of samurais that were constantly  in conflict with each other there wasn't  much of a society or an economy but  Emperor Meiji was determined to change  this he wanted to implement a practical  Imperial Rule and focus on economic  growth and Enlightenment one of the  first things he did was abolish Japanese  domains which were basically the Estates  of super powerful landlords within the  Empire instead he gave these powerful  landlords positions as Governors within  the new Empire Emperor meijiya also  moved the eliminated clashes between  various armies and clans he emerged all  the armies into one Japanese military  and mandated that all Japanese men must  serve at least four years in the armed  forces he figured that fighting side by  side would help them put aside their  differences and work together and he was  right the entire area became more united  than ever and a capitalistic ideologies  were starting to spread Emperor Meiji  even went as far as establishing a  National Assembly and allowed Japanese  citizens to vote clearly even though  Meiji was an emperor by name he wasn't  what you would normally associate with  Emperors he wasn't a power hungry man  that was only concerned about increasing  his own wealth and well-being no he  truly wanted to revolutionize Japan and  to make the Empire a force to be  reckoned with but while Meiji had all of  these crazy Ambitions there was one  thing that he was never willing to do  which was give up Japanese Independence  whether that be political Independence  or energy Independence this did not mean  that he blocked out the West altogether  though he was very much willing to  listen to their ideas and to take their  advice but he wanted all growth to  originate from Japan itself and that's  exactly what happened as the textile and  railroad Industries grew so did the core  production history during meiji's Reign  Japan's silk exports Grew From 646 tons  to 9462 tons but their core production  grew even more from 0.66 million tons to  23.5 million tons by the time Emperor  Meiji died in 1912 the Japanese empire  had become the most developed nation in  East Asia and that this kind of got to  their head they would provoke the United  States by bombing Pearl Harbor and that  didn't really end all that well for them  by the end of World War II Japan was  devastated and occupied by Americans but  while Japan may have been physically  destroyed their spirit and pride was  still very much intact they may have  lost the conquest War but they sure as  hell weren't gonna lose the economic war  and fortunately for them the West was  very much on their side having  capitalistic success stories in the East  with the Japan and South Korea Ria was  very much important for the west and  their war against socialist China and  the USSR so the West supported Japan all  the way and before you knew it the  country was booming but there was one  minor issue Japan's natural resources  could not keep up with the  west-supported steroid growth if this  was during regular times Japan had  probably would have considered more  sustainable options but tensions were  high with the Cold War and Japan had way  too much to prove so in 1960 the  Japanese government allowed imported oil  to compete with the domestic Cola as a  prime source of energy and at this  marked the beginning of Japan's energy  dependence  foreign  throughout the 1960s Japan would  experience some of the most explosive  economic growth numbers ever recorded  their energy dependence also skyrocketed  from 42 to over 90 percent but very few  people saw this as a problem many people  actually saw this as an advantage you  see a Japanese core production was  extremely expensive relative to the US  and Europe due to poor mining conditions  so importing oil from Middle Eastern  countries not only saw the Japan's  energy bottleneck but it also reduced  Energy prices substantially at first  this seemed like a win-win scenario up  until 1973 when Japan first witnessed  the horrors of energy dependence this  year OPEC would introduce an oil embargo  against the US and all of their allies  which of course included Japan with  Rising tensions regarding Israel Japan  and Europe knew that this could be a  possibility and that that's why they  stocked up on oil before the Embargo but  even with that oil prices in Japan grew  four to five fold for perspective the  gas price went up that we saw earlier  this year was barely at 2x from the lows  fortunately this oil embargo will only  lasted about five months but Japan was  very much scarred for life and their  tone regarding economic growth flipped  on its head from maximizing growth no  matter what to just achieving moderate  sustainable growth but this would turn  out to be nearly impossible the energy  consumption of Japan had become so large  that there was no way for the country to  support themselves based on traditional  fuel sources like oil and coal so they  had to think outside the box and to  brainstorm newer methods of energy  generation and what they landed on was  nuclear power Japan had already doubled  with the nuclear power in the past  having opened their first plant in 1966  but after 1973 expanding nuclear power  generation became their number one  priority this was by no means a fast  process but Japan was determined they  consistently opened one plant after  another and within the next 20 years  Japan was able to grow the nuclear power  generation from 3000 megawatts to 43 000  megawatts this brought down their energy  dependence from over 90 percent down to  78 percent there was obviously still a  long way to go and a full-on transition  would likely take a century but Japan  was committed and persistent for them  this wasn't no longer a matter of just  keeping Energy prices low it was a  matter of maintaining economic and  political autonomy you know and never a  good thing when a foreign power can pull  the plug on your entire country but all  of this progress would be stopped at  dead in its tracks in 2011. this year  Japan would experience the worst  earthquake they ever recorded which  caused the tsunami as well the nuclear  power plants would automatically shut  down as soon as it detected the  earthquake but the subsequent tsunami  would flood the Fukushima planned and  the lead to Radioactive contamination  over the next few days 110  000 people had to be evacuated from  nearby areas and the Fukushima disaster  is ranked as the second worst nuclear  disaster in history only beaten out by  Chernobyl I don't think you'd be  surprised to hear that public sentiment  turned strongly against nuclear power  following this incident and with all  this opposition Japan had no choice but  to shut down their remaining plans  making them more energy dependent than  ever  foreign  energy dependence of well over 90  percent it's not surprising that Japan  has to deal with conserving power and  the blackouts despite being a developed  country Japan hasn't quit trying to  become self-sustainable though they've  hatched up a new idea involving hydrogen  instead of nuclear power it turns out  that while Japan doesn't have a much  coal or oil they have a bunch of  hydrogen the ocean floor surrounding  Japan are filled with it this is by no  means a new discovery the reason that  these reserves weren't already leveraged  was due to technological limitations you  see it the ocean floors aren't filled  with hydrogen itself they're actually  filled with a sherbet-like substance  that's often referred to it as fire ice  to extract this fire eyes you first have  to Voyage deep into the ocean where the  depth is over a thousand meters or  nearly 3 300 feet from there you have to  drill hundreds of meters into the sea  floor to actually extract the fire ice  it's not not just plug and play from  there either you then have to extract  the methane inside the fire ice and then  convert it to hydrogen on the bright  side each cubic meter of methane hydrate  is able to produce 160 cubic meters of  methane gas which can then be converted  to hydrogen so you definitely get a  bunch of bang for your buck but that  doesn't make it any easier to extract  the Japanese have been working on a new  drilling method that can transport the  fire eyes through underwater pipelines  directly to Japan but that's still very  much in the beta phases of development  figuring all of this out is only half  the battle though the second half is  actually getting people to use it it  looks like they've definitely got  Japanese companies on board given that  Toyota Honda and the Nissan are all  working on hydrogen vehicles but it's  not clear if Japanese citizens will  actually choose these offerings over  traditional EVS especially given that  hydrogen vehicles and hydrogen itself is  rather expensive fortunately Japan isn't  betting at all on hydrogen they were  very much working on bringing back  nuclear power given that it's been over  a decade since the Fukushima nuclear  disaster tensions have significantly  cooled down and the public has become  much more open to the idea of nuclear  power especially when they see the  blackouts firsthand with winter around  the corner in the northern hemisphere  people are likely going to turn up  heaters which will only worsen the issue  this is why Japan's prime minister is  pushing to reopen as many as nine  nuclear reactors this winter but while  that could help with getting through the  winter Japan will still very much be in  an energy crunch for the foreseeable  future and that's why Japan is  struggling to keep the lights on bu  supported nuclear energy in Japan  scenario comment that down below also  drop a like if you hope that Japan  figures something out for the sake of  their citizens and of course consider  checking out our Discord Community to  suggest for the video ideas and consider  subscribing to see more questions  logically answered but until then I'm  Hari and I'll see you guys on the next  one

